{{- /*
  git/commit-scoped — narrow the commit surface to the files this
  conversation actually touched, ignoring anyone else's uncommitted work
  that may coexist in the working tree.

  Callers should reference this fragment when the current session has
  meaningful history (e.g. gated by `{{ if .Session.HasMessages }}`).
  It emits a short procedure (no headers) so the caller controls the
  surrounding section numbering.
*/ -}}
This conversation has been running for a while — focus the commits on the work
**we did together**, not on unrelated changes that may already exist in the
working tree.

1. Review the conversation so far and enumerate the files we actually touched
   (edits, creations, deletions, renames). Prefer explicit tool traces (edits,
   writes, deletes) over guessing.
2. Run `git status --porcelain` and intersect it with that list — the resulting
   set is the **only** set of files you may propose for commits.
3. If files in `git status` are **not** in that set (someone else's uncommitted
   work, prior WIP, unrelated in-flight changes), leave them alone: do **not**
   `git add` them, do **not** `git stash` them, do **not** `git checkout` them.
   Mention them briefly in the plan under an "Ignoring (not part of this
   conversation)" note so the user knows they were seen and skipped.
4. If our conversation touched a file but `git status` shows it as clean, note
   it (it was likely reverted or already committed) and skip it.
5. Group the in-scope files by scope: feature/component, type (config/docs/
   deps), path (module/directory).
6. Ask if the mapping between the conversation's work and the file set is
   uncertain.
